# Everything here is either regenerated or belongs to whoever is running the
# tool. Nothing about your setup - domains, credentials, certificates - should
# ever reach the repository, so the folder can be cloned or handed on clean.

# Your domain list. domains.example.txt is the shipped sample that setup copies
# from on a first run.
domains.txt

# Checker output, read by ssl-tracker.html as a .js global (see the note at the
# bottom of check-ssl.ps1 for why it is not .json).
ssl-data.js
*.tmp

# Configuration and credentials. secrets.xml is DPAPI-encrypted and bound to one
# Windows user on one machine, but it has no business being committed regardless.
settings.json
secrets.xml
zones.json
alert-state.json
secrets-audit.log

# The audit trail and its rotated archives. Records what happened on THIS
# install - who changed what and when - so it belongs to the operator, not to
# the repository. Never auto-deleted by retention; see Invoke-LogRetention.
audit.log
audit-*.log

# Issued certificates, archived versions, ACME account state. These contain
# UNENCRYPTED PRIVATE KEYS - that is how PEM works, and every ACME client is the
# same. Keep them out of git, and out of any folder that syncs to the cloud.
certs/
acme-state/

# Renewal logs, and the session file naming the current port and token.
jobs/

# The server's own diagnostics. Deliberately outside jobs\ so log retention -
# which only scans jobs\ - can never try to delete a file the running server
# has open. It rotates itself one generation.
server.log
server.1.log

# Posh-ACME, fetched by First Time Setup rather than vendored into the repo.
lib/

# Briefs written for a one-off model-assisted audit and redesign. Working notes
# about the repository rather than any part of the tool.
fable-prompt-*.md

# The jsdom install (~25 MB), and only that. The test SOURCES beside it ARE
# tracked - they are 121 KB, and they record why each fix is correct, which is
# the part that cannot be reconstructed from the code.
#
# This used to be a blanket `tests/`, which achieved the same download but also
# meant a `git clean -fdx` would silently delete every suite - they existed on
# exactly one machine, with no backup. Keeping the download clean and keeping
# the tests in version control turned out to be separable, and they are.
#
# Still development-time only: the app is PowerShell plus static files with no
# build step, and nothing it ships references Node or npm, so a downloaded copy
# runs without installing anything. tests\README.md says how to set them up for
# anyone who wants to run them - it is only `npm install jsdom`.
tests/node_modules/

# The shortcut setup.ps1 creates so the launcher has the camel on it rather than
# the generic batch-file icon. NOT committed: a .lnk bakes in an ABSOLUTE path,
# so a copy made here would point at this machine's folder on somebody else's
# computer and simply fail. Setup rebuilds it wherever the folder ends up.
*.lnk

# Windows junk
desktop.ini
Thumbs.db

# Screenshots for the website. They belong to camelnuggets.com, not to the
# tool - nothing in the app loads them, and committing them would add ~1.3 MB
# to a download that is currently 244 KB.
ScreenShots/

# Private keys generated by new-lb-api-cert.ps1 - the load balancer CA and every
# node certificate issued from it. These are private keys sitting inside the
# tool's own folder, so without this line the first "git add ." commits them.
lb-ca/
